
                        A F U N . A S M


 uchar  nada(uchar c)
 ====================
        Dummy funtion. Returns its input parameter. Usefull for debugging,
 to mark breakpoint.


 void   watchdog(void)
 =====================
        Watchdog function. Needs to be called only when the "atodin"
 function is not invoked.


 void   bell(uchar n)
 =================
        Activates the buzzer n times, where n may range from 0 to 7.
 n =  0:  The buzzer is deactivated.
 n = 1-5: The buzzer beeps 1-5 times.
 n = 6:   The buzzer beeps when a key is depressed.
 n = 7:   The buzzer sounds coutinuously, until the next bell command.


 uchar  putchar(uchar c)
 =======================
        Basic display output function. Prototype in the compiler's "stdio.h"
 include file. The ASCII character "c" is displayed at the current cursor
 position and the cursor is incremented. The function returns its input
 parameter "c".
        The cursor may range from 0 to 7. Positions 0 and 1 point both to
 the leftmost character; position 6 points to the rightmost character, and
 position 7 is the end-of-line mark. When "putchar" is invoked with the
 cursor at 7 (end-of-line), the cursor is not incremented, and the "fscroll"
 flag controls the function's behaviour. If "fscroll" is set, the display
 scrolls to the left, and the character "c" is printed at the rightmost po-
 sition. If "fscroll" is clear, nothing is printed.
        The "c" character is interpreted as follows:
    0     NULL: the display is not modified, the cursor is not incremented.
   1-6    Cursor positionning command: the cursor is set to this value, the
          display is not modified.
    7     Bell: buzzer sounds for 0.1 second, cursor not incremented.
   8-0FH  All equivalent to CR of LF: the cursor goes home (cursor = 1), the
          display is not modified
 10H-1FH  All these characters clear the screen, including the 14 display
          flags. Calling putchar('\0X11'), for example, is the same as
          calling clrscr().
 20H-2DH  Normal ASCII display, if possible (7 segments limitation), or
          blank. The cursor is incremented.
   2EH    Decimal period: displayed but the cursor is not incremented.
 2FH-5FH  ASCII latinic display (upper case). The cursor is incremented.
 60H-79H  ASCII hebrew display, cursor is incremented.
 80H-...  NULL characters: ignored.

 uchar  flag(uchar f, uchar p)
 =============================
        Handles the 14 display flags. "f" is the flag number (from 0 to 13),
 and "p" is the new flag's value. The return value is the state of the flag,
 before the function call; it may be 0 or 1.
        If "p" is 2 or greater, the flag is not modified, the function still
 returns the flag's state (test function).

 void   clrscr(void)
 ===================
        Clears the screen, including the display flags, and homes the cursor.

 bit    kbhit(void)
 ==================
        Similar to the Borland function. It returns true when a key has been
 depressed, at either the parallel or the serial keyboard. After it returns
 true, the "getch" function must be called to get the actual depressed key.
 Unlike the Borland function, it returns false if the depressed key is re-
 leased before "getch" has been called.

 uint   atodin(void)
 ===================
        Samples the A/D converter and returns its 16 bits value, also
 refreshes the watchdog.

 void   recycle(void)
 ====================
        This is a forced jump to test mode. This function may be called from
 the main program. It is (sometimes) called by the error handler function.

 void   relset(uint n)
 =====================
        Fills the relay buffer "relbuf" with the bit pattern defined by the
 input parameter n: all relay with bits set to 1 will be ON.



                          K B D . C


 uchar  getch(void)
 ==================
        Waits for a key to be hit (at any keyboard, parallel or serial), and
 returns its decoded ASCII value.

 void   ungetch(void)
 ====================
        Restore the last character read by "getch", so that the next getch
 call will return it again. A kbhit call will also return true.

 uchar  keypress(void)
 =====================
        Returns the ASCII code of the key being depressed, or 0 if no key
 is being depressed.

 void   switchoff(void)
 ======================
        Switches the indicator off. This function is called by the ON/OFF
 interrupt handler (INT1).


                           I C B U S . C


 void   icini(void)
 ==================
        I2C bus initialization. Must be called once, at least, at power up.

 bit    open(uchar bus_address, uchar memory_address)
 ====================================================
        Opens an I2C device for read/write operation. "bus_address" is the
 the device's I2C bus address, and "memory_address" is the address to a
 device's internal register or memory file. A device must be open before it
 can be read or written into. The function returns a success/fail status.

 void   close(void)
 ==================
        Closes ane open device on the I2C bus. All devices must be closed
 before a new one can be open.

 bit    write(uchar n) small
 ===========================
        Transfers n bytes from the "icbuf" buffer to the actually open
 device. Bytes are written into the device, starting at the memory_address
 used in the "open" call. The function returns a success/fail status.

 bit    read(uchar address, uchar n) small
 =========================================
        Transfers n bytes from the actually open device to the "icbuf"
 buffer. The "address" must be the device's bus_address from the "open"
 call. Bytes are read, starting from the memory_address used in the "open"
 call. The function returns a success/fail status.



                         N O V R A M . C


 bit    novin(uchar *m, uchar p) small
 =====================================
        Reads the NOVRAM page p ("isize" bytes) into the memory, starting
 at address m. If "m" is the NULL pointer, the target destination is the
 "icbuf" buffer itself. Note that "icbuf" is used anyway for the data
 transfer operation (see the icbus read/write functions). The NOVRAM page
 p may range from 0 to 15.

 bit    novout(uchar p, uchar * m) small
 =======================================
        Writes "isize" bytes from the memory file pointed to by "m", into
 the NOVRAM page p. If "m" is the NULL pointer, the "icbuf" buffer itself
 is taken as the source memory.



                         C L O C K . C


 bit    clockin(uchar *m, uchar p) small
 =====================================
        Reads the RTC page p ("isize" bytes) into the memory, starting
 at address m. If "m" is the NULL pointer, the target destination is the
 "icbuf" buffer itself. Note that "icbuf" is used anyway for the data
 transfer operation (see the icbus read/write functions). The RTC page p
 may range from 0 to 7. Page 0 holds the timing registers. Pages 1 to 7
 are general purpose RAM bytes.

 bit    clockout(uchar p, uchar * m) small
 =======================================
        Writes "isize" bytes from the memory file pointed to by "m", into
 the RTC page p. If "m" is the NULL pointer, the "icbuf" buffer itself is
 taken as the source memory.



                         U A R T . C


 void   comini(uchar control) small
 ==================================
        Initialize the internal UART with RS232 parameters set according to
 the control byte "control", which has the following format:
               XXX  BITS  ODD  P-EN  XXX BR2 BR1 BR0
   XXX         Unassigned bit
   BITS        8 bits format if set, 7 bits format if clear.
   ODD         Odd parity if set, even parity if clear
   P-EN        Parity bit appended if set. No parity if clear.
 BR2-BR1-BR0   Baud rate code, ranging from 0 to 7, for the following rates:
     0         300
     1         600
     2         1200
     3         2400
     4         4800
     5         9600
     6         14400
     7         19200

 void   xcomini(uchar control) small
 ===================================
        Initialize the external UART with RS232 parameters set according to
 the control byte "control". Same bit definition as for the internal UART,
 but the baud rate codes are the following:
     0         300
     1         600
     2         1200
     3         2400
     4         4800
     5         9600
     6         19200
     7         38400

 void   flush(void) small
 ========================
        Flushes the internal UART's input buffer.

 void   xflush(void) small
 =========================
        Flushes the external UART's input buffer.

 bit    comstat(void) small
 ==========================
        Returns true if the internal UART's input buffer is not empty.

 bit    xcomstat(void) small
 ===========================
        Returns true if the external UART's input buffer is not empty.

 uchar  getcom(void) small
 =========================
        Waits for a byte reception, at the internal UART, and returns it.
 If the keyboard is activated before reception, the function returns the
 the NULL byte.

 uchar  xgetcom(void) small
 ==========================
        Waits for a byte reception, at the external UART, and returns it.
 If the keyboard is activated before reception, the function returns the
 the NULL byte.

 uchar allstat(void) small
 =========================
        Checks the status of all input devices and returns the following
 value:
                0 - No input from any device
                1 - Internl UART input
                2 - External UART input
                3 - Keyboard input

 uint    allcom() small
 ======================
        Waits for a byte reception at any input device and returns it into
 the least significant byte of integer return value. The most significant
 bit is always cleared, assuming that it can be a parity bit. The device
 number (from the allstat call) is returned into the most significant byte.

 void   comstring(uchar *s) small
 ================================
        Sends the ASCIIZ string s through the internal UART.

 void   xcomstring(uchar *s) small
 ==================================
        Sends the ASCIIZ string s through the external UART.

 void   binstring(uchar *s, uchar n) small
 =========================================
        Sends the n-bytes binary string s, through the internal UART.

 void   xbinstring(uchar *s, uchar n) small
 ==========================================
        Sends the n-bytes binary string s, through the external UART.



                         A T O D . C


 void   wait(uchar n)
 ====================
        Precise delay function: dead wait time of 5 * (n+2) microseconds.
 The delay may be longer if a clock interrupt occurs during the wait time.

 bit    trimset(uchar n) small
 =============================
        Sets the digital trimmer to the value "n", which may range from 0
 to 100. Terminates with an A/D calibration cycle. Returns a success/fail
 status.

 bit    calib(void)
 ==================
        Internal A/D calibration cycle. It must be executed at least once,
 at power up. Note that a "trimset" call also calibrates the A/D. Returns
 a success/fail status.

 uchar  trimfix(uint n)
 ======================
        Attempts to set the digital trimmer so that the A/D reading gets
 the closest possible value under the target "n". During the process, the
 successive trial values are displayed. The final trimmer setting is
 returned as a value ranging from 1 to 99. The value 0 means that the ope-
 ration failed.
